WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.420
Speaker 1: All right. Let's go next to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.140 --> 00:00:10.260
Adrian from San Francisco, where I&nbsp;
just returned from. Adrian from San&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:14.449
Francisco. Welcome. What's on your mind today?
Speaker 2: Good morning, David. How are you?&nbsp;

00:00:14.449 --> 00:00:17.040
Speaker 1: I'm doing well.
Speaker 2: David, I wanted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:26.940
to ask you about Gavin Newsom's proposal for a&nbsp;
constitutional amendment for gun control. And I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:33.660
wanted to talk specifically about the third point,&nbsp;
which is he wanted to ban assault weapons. I was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:39.660
curious what your general perception of a proposal&nbsp;
like that would be, number one in terms of policy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:45.480
but two, just in terms of public sentiment.
Speaker 1: Okay. So the third, let's see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:52.680
I'm looking at the list here of. So raise the&nbsp;
federal minimum age for firearms from 18 to 21.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.400 --> 00:01:00.540
Mandate universal background checks. Institute a&nbsp;
reasonable waiting period for all gun purchases.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.540 --> 00:01:05.820
And I think this is the one you're talking about,&nbsp;
barring civilian purchase of assault weapons that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:09.840
serve no other purpose than to kill as many people&nbsp;
as possible in a short amount of time. You're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:13.260
talking about that last one, right?
Speaker 2: That's right. Thanks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.260 --> 00:01:15.240
for the correction.
Speaker 1: So, listen, he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:21.720
doesn't include that. I can see in this proposal&nbsp;
the exact weapons that would be part of that. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:26.460
this is often a sticking point for the for&nbsp;
the right wing gun people. They go, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:31.080
these Democrats want to ban assault weapons. They&nbsp;
can't even define it. They don't know what it is.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:38.160
I can I can modify a gun so that it technically&nbsp;
is legal, but then I make it function in the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:43.860
way. Ba ba ba ba ba. I think that sort of misses&nbsp;
the forest for the trees. First the question is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.860 --> 00:01:51.000
can the government legally limit the choices&nbsp;
people have when it comes to arms? And I believe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:57.540
everyone says the answer is yes. When you go to&nbsp;
shoulder mounted RPGs, nuclear weapons, grenades,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.540 --> 00:02:02.520
all these different things, almost everyone goes,&nbsp;
well, no. Yeah. Okay. So the government can do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:09.420
some limits. Once you've conceded that, then&nbsp;
it's a matter of where you draw the line and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.420 --> 00:02:13.320
people can disagree about where to draw the&nbsp;
line. But there's a lot of places you could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:18.540
draw the line that wouldn't violate the Second&nbsp;
Amendment in any way. You then get to. Okay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.540 --> 00:02:25.380
well, is this particular gun an assault weapon&nbsp;
or not? That can be figured out. And for me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.380 --> 00:02:31.980
it's not the main sticking point. For me, the&nbsp;
big picture is there are some firearms that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:39.540
truly serve no purpose other than to kill a ton of&nbsp;
people as possible in a short amount of time. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.540 --> 00:02:43.860
it's totally reasonable and logical to decide.&nbsp;
As a society you can have a Second Amendment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.860 --> 00:02:49.980
you have the right to own guns, you can buy guns,&nbsp;
etc., but not any gun. Here are some guns we are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.980 --> 00:02:55.140
going to put on a list. I think that that's fine&nbsp;
and you don't have to know every gun that would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.140 --> 00:03:01.020
be on the list for it to be a valid position.
Speaker 2: Yeah. David, I think that's fair. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.020 --> 00:03:06.660
mean, and total agreement that when Republicans&nbsp;
want to get finicky on the ontology of what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.660 --> 00:03:10.560
classifies as an assault weapon, yeah,&nbsp;
it's totally just to obfuscate. That's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:15.000
entirely their goal. Right. But I guess, you&nbsp;
know, from my very peripheral understanding,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:21.240
like the vast majority of mass shootings are&nbsp;
caused by pistols or handguns. So things that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.240 --> 00:03:28.380
we would not typically nominate or denominate as&nbsp;
assault weapons or a AR 15 or things like that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.380 --> 00:03:33.660
Yeah. So I guess from a public policy perspective,&nbsp;
it seems almost like and I don't want to accuse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.660 --> 00:03:38.250
Gavin of something he's not doing, but it seems&nbsp;
a little bit like a publicity stunt are giving in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.250 --> 00:03:44.040
to sort of public fears and perceptions of which&nbsp;
types of guns are scary as opposed to the type of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.040 --> 00:03:47.100
guns which are actually most commonly used.
Speaker 1: Here's my shot on this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:51.060
Adrian. And you're making a lot of really&nbsp;
good points. I believe that when we talk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.060 --> 00:03:58.980
about gun violence in the United States, we&nbsp;
have three problems. We have suicide. We have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.420 --> 00:04:06.840
what we might call. It's so it's so&nbsp;
tough because mass shooting, I believe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.840 --> 00:04:11.760
is defined as three people killed or injured. So&nbsp;
like if one person is killed and two are injured,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:17.760
that's technically a mass shooting. And then you&nbsp;
have like these sort of nationally newsworthy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.760 --> 00:04:25.020
mass shootings, the ones with eight victims, 12&nbsp;
victims, etc.. You are correct that statistically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.020 --> 00:04:33.420
a much larger share of even what we call mass&nbsp;
shootings are committed with handguns and not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.420 --> 00:04:40.860
the assault weapons. But from what I recall,&nbsp;
it's still around 20 or 25% of mass shootings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.860 --> 00:04:45.720
where there is an assault, what we might call&nbsp;
an assault weapon, a semi-automatic rifle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.720 --> 00:04:49.980
assault weapon type type gun involved, we can fact&nbsp;
check it. Okay. Well, let's put that aside for a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.980 --> 00:04:55.860
second. I think there's a couple different things&nbsp;
going on. Number one, no single provision will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.860 --> 00:05:02.760
solve the gun violence problem. So the waiting&nbsp;
period might do more for the handgun violence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.760 --> 00:05:08.940
the limiting the weapons that are available or&nbsp;
requiring a different type of background check&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.940 --> 00:05:15.120
or whatever. Each of these is going to touch to&nbsp;
different degrees, different types of shootings.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:20.640
And I still think we need to take an all of&nbsp;
the above approach. You can always say this one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.640 --> 00:05:26.340
provision would only deal with 12% of shootings.&nbsp;
Okay. If you've got ten or 12 provisions, each of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.340 --> 00:05:30.600
which will deal with some percentage of shootings,&nbsp;
you're still making a lot of headway. But let me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.600 --> 00:05:36.900
say one other thing, Adrian, and then I'll get&nbsp;
your thoughts. There is also a reality that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.900 --> 00:05:42.360
environment in the United States, because of gun&nbsp;
culture, which includes the school shootings, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.360 --> 00:05:49.620
mall shootings, the theater shootings, all this&nbsp;
different stuff contributes to a really disgusting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.620 --> 00:05:57.360
and negative environment. And I would argue that&nbsp;
on that basis, even if the semiautomatic rifles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.360 --> 00:06:05.760
are only 19 or 22% of the mass shootings,&nbsp;
they generate such terror that it is still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.760 --> 00:06:10.680
worth doing what we can to focus on them while we&nbsp;
also deal with the handguns and the other stuff.&nbsp;

00:06:13.080 --> 00:06:18.300
Speaker 2: I certainly appreciate your very&nbsp;
utilitarian line of thinking. I think that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.300 --> 00:06:23.940
the most that's the de rigueur for public policy.&nbsp;
I guess one sort of point I wonder is, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.940 --> 00:06:32.400
every time there is a media, a hustle bustle about&nbsp;
banning a AR 15 or assault weapons. I think a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.400 --> 00:06:37.860
lot of people go out and buy them. And so do you&nbsp;
think that in doing some sort of publicity stunt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.860 --> 00:06:43.560
because let's be honest, there's no way something&nbsp;
like this will ever reach a constitutional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.560 --> 00:06:49.560
amendment. Yeah. Do you think sort of this sort of&nbsp;
publicity statement, you know, actually encourages&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.560 --> 00:06:52.680
more people to purchase these weapons?
Speaker 1: Yeah, I think it does. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.680 --> 00:06:57.000
there's sort of a there's a double edged sword&nbsp;
to that. If there aren't a lot of shootings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.000 --> 00:07:02.700
then the gun industry and the gun people go, it's&nbsp;
thanks to all the people that bought guns. You&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.700 --> 00:07:08.160
should go out and buy guns. And if there are a lot&nbsp;
of shootings, then the gun people go, Hey, it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.160 --> 00:07:13.020
really dangerous out there. You should go and buy&nbsp;
guns either way. There's a large contingent of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.020 --> 00:07:17.460
gun people that are going to say buying guns and&nbsp;
more guns is the solution. So I don't know that we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.460 --> 00:07:22.800
necessarily want to guide ourselves by that.
Speaker 2: Yeah, that's fair. I can't I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.800 --> 00:07:25.909
can't disagree with that. Well, thank you,&nbsp;
David. I really appreciate the conversation.&nbsp;

00:07:25.909 --> 00:07:31.380
Speaker 1: All right, Adrian from San Francisco&nbsp;
with a great series of questions. Let's take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.380 --> 00:07:34.620
a very quick break and then we'll come&nbsp;
right back and speak to more people If&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.620 --> 00:07:37.920
you're waiting to talk to me. Don't go&nbsp;
anywhere, because we'll be right back.

